and spa at timeshare properties. Hilton Grand Vacations Inc. was founded in 1992 and is headquartered in Orlando, Florida.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Mark D. Wang | CEO & Director | Age 68 | Compensation: $6.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 22K people. Revenue per employee of $226K ranks in the bottom quartile among Consumer Cyclical peers, with revenue growing 12%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $10K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $3K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 28\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (4/10).","financial_summary":["Hilton Grand Vacations Inc. is a mid-cap consumer cyclical business with a $3.3B market cap on $5.0B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +10% over the past three years, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 9% against a 7% cost of capital, which means the business barely earns its keep economically.","The business throws off $230M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 5 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 3.70\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 5.7\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 1.6\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Management returns a meaningful 18.0% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 2.2% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.8) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Very elevated short interest (29.7% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against 